# The CAD kernel is NOT a runtime dependency of the 'partcad' wheel - the core
# builds/renders/exports every shape in sandboxed runtimes and carries geometry
# as BREP-byte envelopes. It is a *test* dependency: several unit tests import
# OCP/build123d/cadquery directly to verify geometry (test_geom, test_part_compound,
# test_assembly, test_assembly_serialize, ...), and pytest collection fails without
# them. Pinned to the versions the sandboxes use (see sandbox_versions.py).
cadquery-ocp==7.9.3.1.1
build123d==0.11.1
ocpsvg==0.6.0
# Every optional extra the 'partcad' wheel declares. Neither is a runtime
# dependency - each carries a client library the core imports lazily, and only
# when the feature that needs it is switched on - but 'tests/partcad/unit' has
# tests that switch exactly those features on and drive them against local mock
# servers ('test_cache_backends.py' for both cache tiers, and 'pc lint' for
# ruff). Without the extra the feature reports itself unusable and skips, the
# tier under test never runs, and the assertion fails on the empty result rather
# than on anything the test meant to check. That is what CI hit: every Pytest
# job on every platform failed 'test_memcache_round_trips_the_payload_untouched'
# with 'assert {} == {"part": True}' and the reason only in the captured log
# ("cacheRemote ... needs the 'aiomcache' package, which is not installed").
#
# Both, not just the one that was missing: 'lint' happens to arrive today
# because '.github/actions/setup-all' installs '.[lint]', but that is
# the workflow's business and not something the test dependencies should rest
# on. Named here, the suite states what it needs to run.
#
# 'memcache' is absent from this list because it is no longer an extra: the
# 'aiomcache' those tests need is an ordinary dependency of 'partcad' now, so
# installing the package at all brings it.
#
# Included by reference rather than restated, so the version ranges a user
# installing 'partcad[aws]' gets are the ones CI tests against. Paths are
# relative to this file, which is what pip resolves '-r' against.
-r requirements-lint.txt
-r requirements-aws.txt
